"source" is just a variable name. The line

const source = items.filter(Boolean);

creates a new array (assigned to the constant source) containing only the truthy entries from the items array. Passing the built-in Boolean function to Array.prototype.filter causes each element to be coerced to true/false; filter keeps the elements for which Boolean(element) is true.

Falsy values removed include: false, 0, "", null, undefined, and NaN. filter returns a new array, and because source is declared with const you cannot reassign the variable (though you could still mutate the array's contents).

Example:
const items = [0, 1, "", "foo", null, undefined, false, true];
const source = items.filter(Boolean);
// source is [1, "foo", true]
